+# Genuine Linux descriptor engine component harness1557
+
+This artifact compiles on Windows only; it has NOT been executed on Linux. Root owns staging, runtime build, CI dispatch and result acceptance. No W/Git/CI mutation is performed here.
+
+Stage the complete source tree as `tools/Validation/Erlang.DescriptorPollValidation/`. Build its project with `-p:RuntimeReferenceDirectory=<existing four-DLL runtime output> -p:EngineVersion=green1558` (or old1554), a real Debug/Release configuration, and distinct output folders. Repository-relative RuntimeReferenceDirectory is resolved from the containing repository root. The friend-enabled output assembly name is Erlang.AsyncMicrostateValidation.dll. No runtime/provider is fabricated or built as fallback.
+
+CLI: `dotnet <output>/Erlang.AsyncMicrostateValidation.dll all <report.json>` runs10 independent genuine green component cases and retains all failures; exit0 requires all pass. `... lost-wake <report.json>` selects only the deterministic old/new interleaving. Old1554 is expected to report an actual three-second pending-request timeout and exit1, then complete real owner/service cleanup. Do not count any other old failure or forced process cleanup as the expected RED. Run the SAME harness and minimal observation patch for old/new; preserve both source versions and raw reports.
+
+The only engine instrumentation is four identical finite insertions: optional concrete probe field, optional constructor argument, field assignment and call after a native EMPTY snapshot is constructed but before actual poll. The concrete probe is one-shot, per engine and host-released outside all engine locks. It cannot settle an engine request, change returned events, or synthesize a native return. Host admits a genuine wait and cancels its actual token while that old empty snapshot is held. Releasing the probe lets actual native poll consume its genuine wake. Old1554 can then lose the new canceled wait; green1558's all-current-terminal pass must settle it before any unrelated input or owner/service stop. The probe is released before cleanup and disposed only after physical thread lifetime ends.
+
+Other controls verify actual poll0 unmanaged record and matching owned OS TID; real pipe read/write readiness; actual cancellation while kernel poll contains that exact request; two owner objects on the same borrowed FD; owned retired-generation FD reuse; native NVAL/EBADF; actual HUP/readEOF; service stop with active kernel snapshot and actual thread join; and dispose before Start with a proven Unstarted outcome. No arbitrary unrelated exceptions are accepted. Original primary and all independent cleanup errors are retained.
+
+On Linux x64 the harness reads only its own actual exported engine thread's `/proc/self/task/TID/syscall` and requires syscall7/poll plus expected native descriptor count (wake-only1, one request2, two requests3). The architecture restriction is explicit. The mapping was checked in the primary kernel v6.12 syscall table: https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.12/arch/x86/entry/syscalls/syscall_64.tbl . These are test-only observation polls; readiness itself is produced exclusively by the engine's real kernel poll. Missing/protected proc state fails rather than manufacturing a witness.
+
+The FD-reuse fixture never selects an arbitrary closed integer: it retains ownership of the original open pipe read FD, awaits old engine-owner retirement, then dup3 replaces that STILL-OWNED target from a new owned pipe. This proves stale registration isolation after the supported physical fence. It does not prove safety when external code closes/reuses an active borrowed FD. Engine never duplicates or changes supplied descriptor flags.
+
+Each fresh component case has real RuntimeMicrostateAccounting and a genuine OS thread/pipe engine; it does not claim ProcessRuntime bootstrap integration or original OTP port behavior equality. Existing DescriptorIoCases/DescriptorIoNames/PortDescriptorCases remain the future public-API regression source; no duplicate Erlang fixture family is created. No blocking data-transfer cancellation, whole statistics compatibility, native exactly-once counts or performance gain is claimed. Compile success is not Linux ABI/race/cleanup/OTP acceptance.
